        // but NETM is onlyOwner, so we must call NETM from the owner. The deployer is address(this) in setUp,
        // however in runtime the test contract uses vm.prank to impersonate the owner. We ensure the owner is this
        // test contract by calling transferOwnership to the fuzzed caller and then back as needed. Simpler: we
        // determine current owner by calling NETM only from address(this) (which is the deployer/owner from new C()).

        // Ensure some observable difference: before calling NETM, balances(owner) may be equal to totalDistributed
        // as in reference; after calling NETM, reference sets balances(owner)=totalDistributed while mutant sets 0.

        // Call NETM as the owner (the deployer of C in setUp is this test contract). Use try/catch to call and capture revert
        bool ok;
        try c.NETM() {
            ok = true;
        } catch {
            ok = false;
        }

        // Single assertion reading contract's public state: the invariant we assert is
        // balances(owner) == totalDistributed must hold after NETM in the reference.
        // We get owner by reading the public owner? owner is not public, so we cannot read it directly.
        // But NETM sets balances[msg.sender], and NETM is onlyOwner, and the only admissible caller is the deployer (this test contract)
